{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and released in 2010, is far more than a simple biopic about the creation of facebook. it’s a sharp, compelling, and deeply unsettling exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the corrosive power of legal battles. it’s a film that lingers in the mind long after the credits roll, prompting questions about the nature of connection, the cost of success, and the blurred lines between personal and professional identity.\n\nthe narrative centers on mark zuckerberg (a brilliant and nuanced performance by edward norton), the socially awkward and intensely driven creator of facebook. the film unfolds primarily through a series of legal proceedings, interweaving them with flashbacks to the events leading up to the platform’s launch. this structure is highly effective, creating a sense of suspense and intrigue as the audience pieces together the story alongside the lawyers and the defendants.\n\nthe performances are uniformly excellent. norton perfectly captures zuckerberg’s cold intensity, his almost unsettling detachment, and his relentless pursuit of innovation. zac efron delivers a surprisingly sympathetic portrayal of eduardo salas, the initial programmer who was ousted from facebook and subsequently sued by zuckerberg. armie hammer as christopher hess, one of the early facebook developers, provides a vital counterpoint to zuckerberg’s ruthlessness, embodying a more idealistic and collaborative spirit. the supporting cast, including jon hamm as greg brady and joe gordon-lewis as the eccentric and often frustrating david solber, are equally compelling, adding layers of complexity to the story.\n\nfincher’s direction is masterful. the film’s visual style is sleek and modern, reflecting the digital world that facebook represents. the use of close-ups, particularly of zuckerberg’s face, effectively conveys his emotional state and inner turmoil. the soundtrack, featuring a blend of electronic music and classical pieces, is perfectly curated to enhance the film’s atmosphere and emotional impact. the editing is tight and precise, keeping the pace brisk and engaging.\n\nthe film’s thematic depth is one of its greatest strengths. it’s not just about the creation of a revolutionary social media platform; it’s about the human cost of ambition. it explores the themes of friendship, loyalty, and betrayal, examining how these relationships are strained and ultimately destroyed in the pursuit of success. it also raises questions about the nature of identity in the digital age and the ways in which technology can both connect and isolate us. the film isn't afraid to be cynical, suggesting that the pursuit of power and wealth can corrupt even the noblest intentions.\n\nwhile the film’s portrayal of the characters can be seen as somewhat reductive, it’s ultimately a compelling and thought-provoking portrait of a pivotal moment in technological history. the social network isn’t just a story about facebook; it’s a story about the human condition, about the complexities of relationships, and about the price we’re willing to pay for success. it’s a film that demands to be seen and discussed, and one that will stay with you long after the lights come up. it’s a triumph of filmmaking and a significant contribution to the cultural conversation surrounding social media.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.9215933084487915}, {"token": "▁social", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "▁network", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.11325513571500778}, {"token": "▁directed", "logprob": -0.002525594551116228}, {"token": "▁by", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "▁david", "logprob": -0.00627156300470233}, {"token": "▁fin", "logprob": -8.106198947643861e-06}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -7.259582343976945e-05}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -0.